Could add a `#[cfg(test)]` module in `lib.rs`:

```rust
#[cfg(test)]
pub mod test_constants {
    pub const TEST_TSPEC_SUFFIX: &str = ".xt.toml";
}
```

Then tests use `crate::test_constants::TEST_TSPEC_SUFFIX`.

Only compiled during tests, single definition, accessible from all test modules in the crate.

Since it's already namespaced in `test_constants`, use a short name:

```rust
#[cfg(test)]
pub mod test_constants {
    pub const SUFFIX: &str = ".xt.toml";
}
```

Then in tests:
```rust
use crate::test_constants::SUFFIX;
```

Reads fine - context is clear. Or even `EXT` if you prefer shorter.

Yes, just `SUFFIX` (or `EXT`) after the `use` statement. I'd go `SUFFIX` to mirror `TSPEC_SUFFIX` in the main code.

So the full picture:

```rust
// lib.rs
pub const TSPEC_SUFFIX: &str = ".xt.toml";

#[cfg(test)]
pub mod test_constants {
    pub const SUFFIX: &str = ".xt.toml";
}
```

```rust
// in any test module
use crate::test_constants::SUFFIX;

#[test]
fn my_test() {
    assert!(filename.ends_with(SUFFIX));
}
```

Want me to implement?

